1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,509
<font color="#FFFFFF">How robust can any classifier possibly be against
an adversary that perturbs inputs at test time?</font>

2
00:00:06,589 --> 00:00:10,339
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper answers that question for the
multi-class setting.</font>

3
00:00:10,419 --> 00:00:15,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors derive achievable information-theoretic
lower bounds on the 0-1 loss of the</font>

4
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:19,065
<font color="#FFFFFF">best possible classifier under a test-time attacker,</font>

5
00:00:19,145 --> 00:00:20,634
<font color="#FFFFFF">for any discrete dataset.</font>

6
00:00:20,714 --> 00:00:26,284
<font color="#FFFFFF">Their framework builds a conflict hypergraph from
the data and the attacker's constraints,</font>

7
00:00:26,364 --> 00:00:31,809
<font color="#FFFFFF">then solves a linear program whose optimum is the
lowest loss any classifier can attain.</font>

8
00:00:31,889 --> 00:00:35,198
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because the exact problem is often too large to solve,</font>

9
00:00:35,278 --> 00:00:39,970
<font color="#FFFFFF">they introduce efficient bounds that pin down the
range of the optimal loss,</font>

10
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:45,368
<font color="#FFFFFF">and use them to reveal a striking gap between
today's adversarially trained models and</font>

11
00:00:45,448 --> 00:00:47,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">what is theoretically achievable.</font>

12
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:53,559
<font color="#FFFFFF">Determining whether a classifier is truly robust to
adversarial examples requires knowing</font>

13
00:00:53,639 --> 00:00:55,586
<font color="#FFFFFF">the best that is even possible.</font>

14
00:00:55,666 --> 00:01:00,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">For binary classification, prior work characterized
this optimal robust loss,</font>

15
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:04,086
<font color="#FFFFFF">giving a reference point to measure progress against.</font>

16
00:01:04,166 --> 00:01:09,055
<font color="#FFFFFF">But real problems have many classes, and the
multi-class case was left open.</font>

17
00:01:09,135 --> 00:01:14,678
<font color="#FFFFFF">There was no way to compute the lowest 0-1 loss
achievable by any classifier against a</font>

18
00:01:14,758 --> 00:01:17,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">test-time attacker on a multi-class dataset,</font>

19
00:01:17,635 --> 00:01:23,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">so practitioners had no way to know how far current
defenses sit from the theoretical limit.</font>

20
00:01:23,950 --> 00:01:29,431
<font color="#FFFFFF">Comparing the robustness of the best possible
classifier to what state-of-the-art training</font>

21
00:01:29,511 --> 00:01:31,532
<font color="#FFFFFF">achieves is a powerful diagnostic.</font>

22
00:01:31,612 --> 00:01:38,452
<font color="#FFFFFF">It tells you whether the bottleneck is your
training method or a fundamental limit of the data and threat model.</font>

23
00:01:38,532 --> 00:01:41,294
<font color="#FFFFFF">Past work delivered this only for two classes.</font>

24
00:01:41,374 --> 00:01:46,113
<font color="#FFFFFF">Moving to many classes is not a trivial extension:
with three or more classes,</font>

25
00:01:46,193 --> 00:01:51,427
<font color="#FFFFFF">examples can interact in higher-order ways that
binary analysis simply cannot capture,</font>

26
00:01:51,507 --> 00:01:55,690
<font color="#FFFFFF">and these interactions can change what optimal
robustness looks like.</font>

27
00:01:56,070 --> 00:01:59,880
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three contributions.</font>

28
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:03,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

29
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:07,660
<font color="#FFFFFF">it generalizes the conflict-graph framework from
binary to multi-class classification,</font>

30
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:11,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing the optimal 0-1 loss is the solution of a
linear program built on a conflict hypergraph.</font>

31
00:02:11,630 --> 00:02:15,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, because that exact program can become
computationally prohibitive,</font>

32
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:19,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">it develops several more efficient bounds, both
lower and upper,</font>

33
00:02:19,410 --> 00:02:23,220
<font color="#FFFFFF">that bracket the range in which the true optimal
loss must lie.</font>

34
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:27,110
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, it delivers an extensive empirical study,</font>

35
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:31,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">giving the first analysis of the gap to optimal
robustness for classifiers in the multi-class</font>

36
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:34,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">setting on benchmark datasets.</font>

37
00:02:35,270 --> 00:02:40,396
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core idea is to represent the classification
problem as a conflict hypergraph.</font>

38
00:02:40,476 --> 00:02:42,174
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each vertex is a data point,</font>

39
00:02:42,254 --> 00:02:47,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a set of points forms a hyperedge when they
belong to different classes yet share</font>

40
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:49,983
<font color="#FFFFFF">overlapping adversarial neighborhoods,</font>

41
00:02:50,063 --> 00:02:54,490
<font color="#FFFFFF">meaning an attacker could push any of them to the
same confusing input.</font>

42
00:02:54,570 --> 00:03:00,458
<font color="#FFFFFF">The optimal 0-1 loss then becomes a linear program:
maximize the probability mass of correctly</font>

43
00:03:00,538 --> 00:03:05,918
<font color="#FFFFFF">classified points subject to constraints encoded by
the hypergraph's incidence matrix.</font>

44
00:03:05,998 --> 00:03:10,235
<font color="#FFFFFF">The dual program is a fractional covering of
vertices by hyperedges,</font>

45
00:03:10,315 --> 00:03:13,791
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it directly yields the optimal adversarial strategy.</font>

46
00:03:13,871 --> 00:03:17,854
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because computing all hyperedges up to degree ten
is infeasible,</font>

47
00:03:17,934 --> 00:03:22,933
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors truncate the hypergraph to lower
degrees for tractable lower bounds,</font>

48
00:03:23,013 --> 00:03:27,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">aggregate binary one-versus-one losses for an even
cheaper bound,</font>

49
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:31,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">and apply a generalized Caro-Wei bound for a
matching upper bound.</font>

50
00:03:31,630 --> 00:03:36,280
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments use two standard vision benchmarks,
MNIST and CIFAR-10,</font>

51
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:41,942
<font color="#FFFFFF">under an L-two constrained attacker evaluated
across a sweep of perturbation budgets.</font>

52
00:03:42,022 --> 00:03:47,471
<font color="#FFFFFF">For the 3-class studies they take one thousand
samples per class, using digits one,</font>

53
00:03:47,551 --> 00:03:52,600
<font color="#FFFFFF">four, and seven for MNIST and the plane, bird, and
ship classes for CIFAR-10.</font>

54
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:58,262
<font color="#FFFFFF">They also compute bounds for the full 10-class
problem on the complete training sets.</font>

55
00:03:58,342 --> 00:04:03,524
<font color="#FFFFFF">As reference defenses, they train classifiers with
TRADES adversarial training,</font>

56
00:04:03,604 --> 00:04:08,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">a small convolutional network for MNIST and a wide
residual network for CIFAR-10,</font>

57
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">and evaluate them with the strong APGD attack from
AutoAttack.</font>

58
00:04:13,430 --> 00:04:17,355
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline finding is a large, previously
unquantified gap.</font>

59
00:04:17,435 --> 00:04:22,673
<font color="#FFFFFF">Adversarially trained classifiers perform far worse
than the theoretical optimum,</font>

60
00:04:22,753 --> 00:04:28,122
<font color="#FFFFFF">and this gap is much wider than what prior work
observed for binary classification.</font>

61
00:04:28,202 --> 00:04:29,435
<font color="#FFFFFF">On 3-class CIFAR-10,</font>

62
00:04:29,515 --> 00:04:35,213
<font color="#FFFFFF">TRADES adversarial training cannot achieve a loss
much better than 0.6 at a perturbation</font>

63
00:04:35,293 --> 00:04:39,349
<font color="#FFFFFF">strength where the optimal achievable loss is
essentially zero.</font>

64
00:04:39,429 --> 00:04:40,924
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the 10-class setting,</font>

65
00:04:41,004 --> 00:04:47,752
<font color="#FFFFFF">the efficient lower and upper bounds sandwich the
optimal loss tightly for the budgets used in practice,</font>

66
00:04:47,832 --> 00:04:50,772
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the gap is not an artifact of loose bounds.</font>

67
00:04:50,852 --> 00:04:56,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">This suggests current robust training struggles far
more as the number of classes grows.</font>

68
00:04:56,930 --> 00:05:00,821
<font color="#FFFFFF">A key ablation examines how much the higher-order
hyperedges actually matter.</font>

69
00:05:00,901 --> 00:05:04,792
<font color="#FFFFFF">Surprisingly,</font>

70
00:05:04,872 --> 00:05:08,763
<font color="#FFFFFF">at small perturbation budgets the lower bound
computed with only edges is nearly identical</font>

71
00:05:08,843 --> 00:05:12,734
<font color="#FFFFFF">to bounds that add degree-three and degree-four
hyperedges,</font>

72
00:05:12,814 --> 00:05:16,705
<font color="#FFFFFF">even though the graph contains millions of these
higher-order structures.</font>

73
00:05:16,785 --> 00:05:20,675
<font color="#FFFFFF">For CIFAR-10 at budget three,</font>

74
00:05:20,755 --> 00:05:24,646
<font color="#FFFFFF">there are roughly three million degree-three and
ten million degree-four hyperedges,</font>

75
00:05:24,726 --> 00:05:28,617
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet they have no impact on the computed bound.</font>

76
00:05:28,697 --> 00:05:32,588
<font color="#FFFFFF">This means edge-only bounds are both cheap and
accurate in the practical regime.</font>

77
00:05:32,668 --> 00:05:36,559
<font color="#FFFFFF">The aggregated binary bound is the fastest to
compute but much looser,</font>

78
00:05:36,639 --> 00:05:40,530
<font color="#FFFFFF">and scaling up model architecture yields only minor
gains at low budgets.</font>

79
00:05:40,910 --> 00:05:45,032
<font color="#FFFFFF">The numbers make the gap concrete.</font>

80
00:05:45,112 --> 00:05:49,233
<font color="#FFFFFF">On 3-class CIFAR-10,</font>

81
00:05:49,313 --> 00:05:53,435
<font color="#FFFFFF">adversarial training plateaus near 0.6 loss at a
budget where the optimal loss is essentially zero.</font>

82
00:05:53,515 --> 00:05:57,637
<font color="#FFFFFF">State-of-the-art certifiably robust models fare no
better against the optimum: the best</font>

83
00:05:57,717 --> 00:06:01,838
<font color="#FFFFFF">MNIST model has 0-1 loss of 0.27 at budget
one-point-five-two and 0.44 at budget two,</font>

84
00:06:01,918 --> 00:06:06,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">while the achievable optimal lower bound is zero in
both cases.</font>

85
00:06:06,120 --> 00:06:10,242
<font color="#FFFFFF">On CIFAR-10,</font>

86
00:06:10,322 --> 00:06:14,443
<font color="#FFFFFF">the best certified model reaches 0.6 loss at budget
one and 0.8 at budget two,</font>

87
00:06:14,523 --> 00:06:18,645
<font color="#FFFFFF">again against an optimal lower bound of zero.</font>

88
00:06:18,725 --> 00:06:22,847
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on the structural side, millions of
higher-degree hyperedges,</font>

89
00:06:22,927 --> 00:06:27,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">three million of degree three and ten million of
degree four at budget three,</font>

90
00:06:27,128 --> 00:06:31,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">leave the bound completely unchanged.</font>

91
00:06:31,630 --> 00:06:36,751
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is that multi-class robust
classification has a large and now-measurable</font>

92
00:06:36,831 --> 00:06:41,463
<font color="#FFFFFF">gap between what current defenses achieve and what
is theoretically possible,</font>

93
00:06:41,543 --> 00:06:44,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">a gap that is far worse than in the binary case.</font>

94
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:50,029
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper's conflict-hypergraph framework computes
the optimal 0-1 loss as a linear program,</font>

95
00:06:50,109 --> 00:06:57,188
<font color="#FFFFFF">and its efficient truncated bounds pin that optimum
down tightly using only edges in the practical low-budget regime.</font>

96
00:06:57,268 --> 00:07:03,124
<font color="#FFFFFF">This gives practitioners a fast diagnostic tool to
see how much robustness is still on the table,</font>

97
00:07:03,204 --> 00:07:09,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it points future research toward closing the
gap rather than endlessly iterating attacks and defenses.</font>
